\f0\fs36 \cf0 This book explores programming topics from the high level (with BASIC, limited as it is, is still a high-level language) as well as the low level.  Yes, there will be 6502 assembly!\
\
Before you run away screaming about assembly language, here's my thought process: \
\
1) I wish I had learned this stuff way back when. I could have really programmed some cool stuff, made some cool games, and generally would be cooler understanding the machine deeper. (I guess you can imagine that being cool was a big motivation)\
\
2) 6502 assembly language isn't really that hard. It's just kind of verbose. "move a byte to the accumulator. Do something with it.  Compare that byte with some other byte. Choose to branch (or not) based on the value of that byte."  Pretty much all the stuff you need for Turing-completeness. \
\
If you've met Javascript and CSS and didn't die in the process, you have the mental chops to learn and enjoy 6502 assembly language.  (now something like writing modern X86 assembly language, that's crazypants, and you should let the compiler do the heavy lifting for you.  If you'd like to learn to read modern assembly language, the Casey Muratori 
\f1\i Computer, Enhance
\f0\i0  course is excellent)\
\
For the most topics, it will be explored from the BASIC-side of the world, and then explored from the assembly-side of the world. Chapter XX-a (like Chapter 5a) is the BASIC-side, and Chapter XX-b (like Chapter 5b) is the assembly side.  I know that the A-chapter is B-asic, and the B-chapter is A-ssembly, but you do 
\f2\b not
\f0\b0  want to attack this stuff assembly-first :-D\
\
There will be projects, but (at least at the time of early early early writing) there will be different projects in the BASIC and Assembly worlds.  Some chapters might not have an assembly-side. Some chapters might not have a BASIC-side.  
\f1\i I have no idea what I'll do with the chapter titles there.  Maybe Chapter 5q for "It's not one or the other, so have a Q."
\f0\i0 \
\
If the thought of tackling assembly language is just not for you, remember that it's your book and your time. No shame in deciding that something isn't worth dealing with right now. It'll still be here, waiting for you.\
